WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.710
It's great to welcome back today, Peter Joseph,
who's the founder of the Zeitgeist movement

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:12.849
and director and writer of the film Inter
Reflections, of course, based on his book.

00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:14.429
Peter, it's so great to have you back on.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:15.429
Thank you, David.

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:16.429
I appreciate you having me.

00:00:16.429 --> 00:00:17.590
Happy doing well.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.760
So I'm curious.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.310
So much has happened over the last four years
and in particular over the last couple of

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:30.519
weeks, really since the November election
with, you know, literally dozens and dozens

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:35.750
of events that previously we would imagine
to be extraordinarily unlikely in the United

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:36.750
States.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.870
I'm curious on a few different levels.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.410
Number one, based on your analysis and the
perspective that you've talked about on our

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.079
program before, that you have about the way
the United States is organized, are the events

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:52.149
of the last few weeks that that unlikely or
is it exactly the type of thing that your

00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:55.809
perspective would would suggest is inevitable
in some sense?

00:00:55.809 --> 00:01:02.999
Well, the specifics are hard to pinpoint,
but this kind of right wing insurrection coming

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:09.609
from what I feel is many, many generations
of lower class oppression isn't necessarily

00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:10.609
unpredictable.

00:01:10.609 --> 00:01:15.500
You know, the there's this kind of a short
term picture and a long term picture, as with

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.760
anything when it comes to to the human condition
in terms of causality and human behavior.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.350
So on one side, if you look at the events
of January 6th, you have this rather sparse

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.280
insurrection, so to speak, kind of a satire
of a coup, if you will, if you really kind

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.359
of look at it when they stormed the capital.

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:40.010
And then you relate that clearly to a figurehead
that has deep cult of personality and has

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:44.479
been promoting things in his self-interest
with no responsibility.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:47.509
And people have flocked to him and they believed
him.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:49.179
And this is where we end up.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:50.200
So that's one perspective.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.149
I don't think you can just blame, for example,
Donald Trump explicitly.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:54.460
Donald Trump came from somewhere.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.460
He was a manifestation of something.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:01.049
I mean, if you look at his character in and
of himself, he represents really a kind of

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:05.079
kind of extreme caricature of what capitalist
society is.

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:06.079
Right.

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:07.079
It's really about people.

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:11.190
I mean, he's really groomed into this since
his father's time and he knows how to angle

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.810
he a professional angler.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.150
And there's a there's a line there with morality
where someone goes from being a good businessman

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:18.630
to being effectively a criminal.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:23.470
And I think there's debate as to how gray
that area is when you're dealing with exploitation

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.660
and gaming, because that's what our society
is.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:26.660
We're gaming.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:27.660
That's what people do.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.920
That's the only theory that really supports
the kind of behavior that our economy does

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.489
is game theory, as people have modeled in
the past in terms of who will be successful

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:36.690
and who will not.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.690
So there's that.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.689
So you have that kind of character that comes
from society and that on the other side you

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:42.689
have people.

00:02:42.689 --> 00:02:46.739
And again, it's sad because people become
so upset when they look at these images and

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:52.960
they see the they see it effectively, a kind
of domestic terrorist heir to this right wing

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.040
insurgency obsessed with conspiracies.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.640
They don't believe anything.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.220
It's easy to be upset with those people, but
they have they came from somewhere.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.310
There's a reason why they have evolved to
where they've become.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.590
And it's not just Donald Trump.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.140
As I said before, I think this society in
America due to socioeconomic inequality is

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:11.939
something that we spoke about years ago in
my book, The New Human Rights Movement.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:14.970
It's the core focus of that book.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.849
Socioeconomic inequality is one of the largest
drivers of destabilization than anything.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:23.890
It is it is the ultimate precondition, so
to speak, sociologically that forces people

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.680
into a kind of antagonism.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.909
Even in a wealthy society such as the United
States, the haves and the have nots have become

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:31.440
so severe throughout the years.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.060
And people to understand why they understand
the mechanics, they understand the history,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:38.459
they start to feel, they start to feel like
they're left behind.

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:44.019
So as bizarre as it is that you have a white
culture in America that now feels they're

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:49.120
oppressed, given the true history of America,
where obviously the white man particularly

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.450
is far from the most oppressed, but this is
manifest from this what I believe, class inequality.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.840
And I think there's a great deal of sociological
research, epidemiological study that supports

00:03:58.840 --> 00:03:59.840
that.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.560
So for me, the solution to all of this is
to create more economic equality.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.959
Ultimately, that's really the most critical
thing society can do at this point to help

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:12.980
it stop the instability that I think is only
going to keep growing at many different levels

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.420
from the standpoint of, well, I'll frame this
up in a slightly different way and get get

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.450
your thoughts on it.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:27.030
Whenever we we've seen over the last four
years the next high bar of crazy reached,

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:32.730
sometimes there will be this feeling of, OK,
this is now over the line and nobody is going

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.230
to be OK with this.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:39.160
And then we hear, oh, actually, there's people
who are OK with it.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:45.130
And then you see the social media posts and
then you see the short memory that that many

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.670
have for these sorts of things.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:53.450
And you see the way that the media apparatus
sort of creates an environment where no matter

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:54.940
what takes place, some.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.220
One is there that you can point to that will
actually say this is actually OK or defended

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:05.030
in some way, and we could if we say what was
the origin of this, we could come up with

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:07.570
a lot of different starting points.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:13.880
But in terms of the political media landscape,
I often think back to the Reagan welfare queen

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:19.040
meme of this imaginary woman who's having
all these kids and getting rich off of it

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.130
and has a Cadillac.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.580
And like once you believe that, then you go
into immigrants are dangerous and then you

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.850
go into trickle down economics or whatever.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:28.850
Right.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:34.250
Like fill in the blanks and then you get to
Hugo Chavez and Dominion and whatever stole

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:39.330
the election, like the ultimate caricature
that we heard over the last couple of months.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:45.240
Is that a fair way to talk about the history
and that that makes it about a 35 year history,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.380
or would you kind of frame it up in a different
way?

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:52.330
Well, I think that you're touching upon how
to things how belief systems come to be and

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.560
belief system share share excuse me, a common
architecture.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:02.430
So going back to Reagan, Thatcher and the
rise of neo liberalism, where Thatcher, I

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.950
believe her quote was there is no society.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.130
She's all about the individual.

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:14.380
And this sort of narcissistic, righteous self
individualism came forward that clearly has

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:15.860
been deeply caustic.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.760
And if you believe things like, well, you
get what you work for or you just have to

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.750
pull yourself up by your bootstraps, if you
believe that people have complete free will

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.890
and it's all up to them, which is that general
gesture of all of this, then you're going

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.640
to not care very much about people that are
actually suffering legitimately because of

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.440
the very structure of the system to not have
the same advantages that other people do because

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.500
of the long, long generation of inequality
in many facets of that.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:40.920
But, yeah, I agree with you.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:41.920
Absolutely.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.460
It's it's in the book I remember writing a
whole section on how the kind of wing mindset

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:54.020
can be created and how, particularly from
that standpoint, how this if you like, for

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.180
example, if you believe that you get what
you work for, then you probably also believe

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:03.030
that people that commit crime have absolutely
total responsibility.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.340
Forget they're there in poverty, forget what
their life condition was.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:10.750
So you end up with that kind of distorted
value system based on narrow individualism.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:15.080
And I think that also is very much part of
this society because of the economic system

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.080
once again.

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.720
So my causality, as you know, David, it's
really all about what our economy has done

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:23.330
to us sociologically and obviously psychologically.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:27.260
And if you have people that are stuck in a
system, regardless of the reality of our condition,

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.490
regardless of our potentials for, say, post
scarcity, and they're fighting with each other

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:35.420
because of the structure, not because we have
to, but because of the structure, you're going

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.510
to have antagonism, you're going to have racism.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:40.060
You're going to have all this group versus
group stuff.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.940
You see it microcosms, you see it macrocosmic.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.990
I was on another podcast the other day and
I was reminded that in West Virginia I'm from

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:51.010
the South and I had friends, a friend of a
friend from West Virginia, and they were describing

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:56.980
to me something surreal and how in West Virginia
before NAFTA, the south and the north actually

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:59.650
were at odds because of economic strife.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:03.140
If you can believe that, just like you saw
it, you see in Italy, in the north and south

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.850
historically as well, which also generated
mob stuff.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:12.330
But it wasn't until NAFTA came along and jobs
were taken by people in the global south that

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.360
people started to hate people from the global
south.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.840
They started to hate Mexicans.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.350
So you see that antagonism as well.

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:20.200
So that's a good corollary.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:25.120
So one of the calls related to this that I
get frequently recently when I when I hear

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.870
from my audience is how do we fix this?

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:35.469
And one of the reactions I've had is if we
grant that it's taken 30 to pick the Reagan

00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:40.959
Thatcher example, 35, 40 years to get into
this, it would be unreasonable to expect that

00:08:40.959 --> 00:08:45.089
in a two, four or six year period of time
we would get out of it.

00:08:45.089 --> 00:08:49.399
And some of the things that have to be changed
are, on the one hand, as you're talking about,

00:08:49.399 --> 00:08:53.180
the sort of structure of the financial system
and inequality.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:59.581
On the other hand, I when people ask me, how
can I convince my aunt that she's wrong, I

00:08:59.581 --> 00:09:04.610
think that that's that's what we might call
a retail strategy and we could have that conversation.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:09.699
But it's less relevant to the fact that you
probably need a decade or longer of cultural

00:09:09.699 --> 00:09:15.069
and an economic and educational change to
really start changing these things.

00:09:15.069 --> 00:09:21.120
It would other than by dictatorship, I don't
know how you achieve the type of change that

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:25.690
has taken place over 40 years in a short period
of time.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:29.360
I, I certainly can sympathize with that sentiment.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.149
I would say on a certain level, the cultural
shifts required, particularly when it comes

00:09:33.149 --> 00:09:37.300
to something like racism, which I do believe
could subside greatly in this country and

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:42.230
beyond, it would take more than that because
racism has a snowball effect.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:50.450
Racism started as a as a legal construct where
in excuse me, the slavery period in early

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.450
America.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.259
And many people don't realize this, the indentured
servants that were imprisoned.

00:09:55.259 --> 00:09:58.759
In Europe, they were taken over here, they
used for about seven years and they could

00:09:58.759 --> 00:10:03.279
be free, that was not the best business investment
from the standpoint of the plantation owners.

00:10:03.279 --> 00:10:08.070
And they started to learn about the African
transatlantic slave trade, where the kings

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.380
in Africa were kidnaping their own people
and literally selling them to the Dutch and

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.420
other other slave ships.

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:17.279
And they were brought over to America as a
new investment.

00:10:17.279 --> 00:10:20.120
But the fact that these people had black skin
had no relevance.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.779
It wasn't until later, in fact, they even
intermarried with indentured servants and

00:10:23.779 --> 00:10:24.779
had kids.

00:10:24.779 --> 00:10:27.389
This is it wasn't this taboo.

00:10:27.389 --> 00:10:32.660
It wasn't until the legal construct of race
was created to fortify racism in and of itself

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:34.660
and create an excuse for it ultimately.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:40.220
And then after the civil war excuse me, after
after the after the after the civil war, but

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:44.410
before that, the legal construct was formally
formalized, even more so.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:49.800
So the poor black people could be subservient
to the poor white people to avoid any kind

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:50.800
of upheaval.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.389
And Dr. Martin Luther King wrote about this
eloquently how the birth of racism came to

00:10:55.389 --> 00:10:56.389
be.

00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:57.389
And it's not what people think it is.

00:10:57.389 --> 00:10:59.579
So now you have families that are racists.

00:10:59.579 --> 00:11:01.029
They've been racist for generations.

00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:03.019
They teach racist values to their kids.

00:11:03.019 --> 00:11:04.139
How do you stop that?

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:08.459
Obviously, that take generations, but the
economic foundation is still there.

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:13.600
And I strongly believe the stress of this
strife and groupism, this group antagonism

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:14.980
of our society is to blame.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.959
And if you can get rid of that, to whatever
degree, it's going to have an equivalent positive

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:20.980
effect on people's bigotry.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:25.279
So to answer your question, I would say to
people, obviously, you need policy shifts

00:11:25.279 --> 00:11:26.630
to create more equality.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:27.630
I am.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:28.839
I'm for de marketization.

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:31.960
I have gone through the gamut in my mind and
analysis.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.080
I'm not a Marxist and I don't subscribe to
anything socialist in any kind of traditional

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:37.080
sense.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:41.800
I look at it from a systems science perspective
and what we know in sociology and of course,

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.779
efficiency when it comes to systems.

00:11:43.779 --> 00:11:48.269
We could go on the tangent of why the system
is unsustainable ecologically and so on.

00:11:48.269 --> 00:11:49.420
That's a big part of it as well.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:55.499
But in terms of this sociology, excuse me,
you have to start giving people what they

00:11:55.499 --> 00:11:56.499
need directly.

00:11:56.499 --> 00:11:59.730
So you should be in favor of something like
UBI as a stepping stone.

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:05.579
People should literally want there to be ultimately
free food and free transportation and a free

00:12:05.579 --> 00:12:06.579
foundation.

00:12:06.579 --> 00:12:08.190
There should be that we have the resources
to do that.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.660
So first, people need to understand that and
they need to understand that if we have our

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:14.509
needs supplied now, I'm not talking about
luxuries and excess.

00:12:14.509 --> 00:12:17.009
We could still have that as a top tier market
reality.

00:12:17.009 --> 00:12:18.009
Right.

00:12:18.009 --> 00:12:21.500
But you give people what they need needs that
no one's in poverty anymore.

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:23.160
Then you're going to see these shifts happen.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.160
And people need to understand that that is
not some kind of inhibition of people's freedom

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.139
and the propaganda that we have or people
just can't even handle that concept.

00:12:31.139 --> 00:12:34.540
We can't even talk about universal health
care in America because someone will inevitably

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.029
attribute it to socialism and then think we're
going to be in some gulag eventually because

00:12:38.029 --> 00:12:39.430
we have universal health care.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:40.430
Right.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:41.430
So you see the point.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.100
But I can't answer that question any kind
of explicit way.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:48.129
I have plenty of proposals that I've talked
about, and it's obviously education.

00:12:48.129 --> 00:12:49.189
But you need to have policy shifts.

00:12:49.189 --> 00:12:55.449
And I will say this eventually through a federalization,
which is this more with less phenomenon.

00:12:55.449 --> 00:13:01.339
It is to me the the only real economic wealth
that we have.

00:13:01.339 --> 00:13:04.079
And it's not wealth in the sense of a physical
good.

00:13:04.079 --> 00:13:07.120
It's wealth in the sense of an extremely efficient
process.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:12.660
Jeremy Rifkin refers it to it as zero marginal
cost in reference to to the current society

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:17.930
where we become so efficient in a given area
where we've created technology that can that

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:21.339
can perform actions such great efficiency,
they become cheaper and cheaper and cheaper

00:13:21.339 --> 00:13:22.510
and smaller and smaller and smaller.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:27.209
You know, the big computer fifty years ago,
we had many tons, took enormous amount of

00:13:27.209 --> 00:13:28.529
energy, cost millions of dollars.

00:13:28.529 --> 00:13:30.629
Now your cell phone chip is more powerful.

00:13:30.629 --> 00:13:36.379
That applied to just about everything in society
economically is where I think localization

00:13:36.379 --> 00:13:42.190
and a return to a kind of community driven,
high tech reality where people get off the

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:44.550
grid of normal state control.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:49.819
I don't mean state in a derogatory way, but
there's the limitations of using a telecom

00:13:49.819 --> 00:13:52.999
company, the limitations of having to buy
your food from a major corporation.

00:13:52.999 --> 00:13:56.750
I believe if people act accordingly and this
is the kind of stuff I get into at the end

00:13:56.750 --> 00:14:00.990
of my my film and Reflections, which begins
what will be the second film.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.560
And I've commented on this in my book.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:07.790
I don't want to go too much detail, but it's
about getting off the grid of the market system

